add(f"The five 1 MiB upload/download cycles took <b>{f(N['seconds'])} seconds</b>. Gateway payload transfer was <b>{f(N['gatewayBytes']/1048576)} MiB</b> at <b>{f(N['gatewayMiBPerSecond'])} MiB/s</b>. Storage nodes received <b>{f(N['storageReceived']/1048576)} MiB</b> across both replicas and served <b>{f(N['storageSent']/1048576)} MiB</b>, giving an aggregate storage-payload rate of <b>{f(N['storageMiBPerSecond'])} MiB/s</b>. These counters exclude headers, Ethereum RPC, TCP/TLS framing and libp2p overhead; they do not measure physical-link capacity.")
sub('What scales and what remains a bottleneck')
add('The transaction-only workloads create synthetic record commitments using pre-existing content; they do not represent complete parallel clinical uploads. The 10, 50 and 100 operation scenarios use 1, 4 and 8 workers respectively. Explicit per-wallet nonces, a fixed load-transaction gas price of 2 gwei and a 350,000 gas cap separate transaction scheduling from gas estimation. Successes and receipt times, rather than submission counts alone, determine TPS.')
add('On-chain create, grant and revoke operations each touch a constant number of mapping entries. Nevertheless, total state grows with records and grants, total history grows with events, and block gas capacity limits throughput. The gateway auditor queue serializes read-audit submissions, while file serialization and redundant pinning add CPU, bandwidth and storage pressure. The browser currently scans all records and retrieves event history; an indexed, paginated service is necessary for large histories.')
add('Jointly changing volume and concurrency limits causal interpretation: a larger TPS does not prove linear scaling with either one alone. Five repeated file trials and three load points are a small experiment. A stronger study would hold volume constant while varying concurrency, repeat each scenario, introduce controlled WAN delay, compare multiple hosts, measure failure recovery and test larger record populations.')

page('12. Challenges, limitations and future improvements')
table(['Issue','Consequence','Priority improvement'],[['Interrupted multi-stage upload','Blockchain and IPFS cannot share one atomic commit; orphan objects or missing envelopes may remain','Durable client upload journal and idempotent recovery'],['Key loss and browser compromise','Non-exportable keys may still be used by malicious same-origin code; lost keys cannot be recovered','Patient-controlled backup, key rotation and device security'],['Revocation after disclosure','A recipient may retain prior plaintext or the AES key','Honest consent semantics; key/version rotation for future record updates'],['Central gateway and auditor','Service outage blocks key delivery; dishonest audit attestations remain possible','Replicated gateway state, controlled relayer keys, independent log anchoring'],['One-host deployment','A host failure affects EVM and both storage peers together','Independent nodes, off-host backups and failure injection'],['Metadata visibility','Observers can link wallets, record activity and consent relationships','Permissioned deployments or privacy-preserving credentials where justified'],['Provider verification','Approval currently relies on manual external checking','Professional identity integration and per-staff hospital roles'],['Small evaluation scope','Local metrics cannot predict clinical/public-chain performance','Larger repeated factorial workloads and independent security review']],[125,192,W-317])
sub('Deployment and reproducibility')
add('The submitted project pins its JavaScript dependencies in package-lock.json and pins the Solidity compiler and EVM target. After installing Node.js, run npm ci and npm start from the project root, then open http://localhost:3000. The initial run deploys the contract and retains the EVM database, both IPFS stores and the envelope index. Tests and benchmarks deploy separate instances. Keep the same browser profile and hostname so that the encryption identity remains available.')
add('An optional script can deploy the contract to an external EVM endpoint using a student-supplied funded test wallet and separate auditor. That deployment was not performed here and does not automatically migrate the local gateway or browser configuration. Default demo wallets and unlocked RPC must remain confined to a synthetic local laboratory. A production design requires HTTPS, protected RPC access, managed keys, rate limits, recovery, off-host infrastructure and appropriate privacy governance.')
sub('Conclusion')
add('MediLedger implements every listed functional area and supplies a repeatable local evaluation across the requested metric categories. Its useful contribution is the combination of patient-signed consent, recipient-specific encrypted keys, integrity-checked off-chain files and inspectable access decisions. Its limitations are equally important: blockchain does not conceal metadata, recover lost keys, recall copied information or remove off-chain trust. The results support an educational prototype, not a production healthcare-security claim.')

page('13. Submission, demonstration and references')
